.TH "HEE-PVE-DEPLOY" "8" "" "" "HEE Tools"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
hee-pve-deploy - deploy and provision a Proxmox LXC from one declarative
manifest
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
hee-pve-deploy [-h] [--node NODE] [--host HOST] [--dry-run]
  hee-pve-deploy MANIFEST.yaml [--node pve] [--host 10.0.0.153] [--dry-run]
                 [--anchors FILE] [--addons FILE] [--agent-roster FILE]

positional arguments:
  manifest              pve/services/<name>.yaml -- see the manifest format
                        above

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --node NODE
  --host HOST
  --dry-run             resolve the manifest and print every command that
                        would run -- create, add-ons, files, provision --
                        creating and changing nothing. Without it the deploy
                        is applied.
  --anchors ANCHORS     registry of roles whose address may be pinned. A
                        manifest that declares address or hwaddr must name a
                        role listed there; anything else has its address
                        allocated.
  --addons ADDONS       the org\[aq]s container-addons registry, the only place an
                        \[aq]addons:\[aq] name resolves. Unreadable means every add-on
                        is refused, never silently skipped.
  --agent-roster AGENT_ROSTER
                        the org\[aq]s signed agent roster, the only place a model
                        is named. Read only by \[aq]generate: agent-roster-model\[aq]
                        files entries; it must be ratified and its .asc must
                        verify, or nothing is rendered.
\f[R]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
                      [--anchors ANCHORS] [--addons ADDONS]
                      [--agent-roster AGENT_ROSTER]
                      manifest

hee-pve-deploy -- deploy and provision a Proxmox LXC from one declarative manifest.

One YAML file per service; this tool creates the container via the real
Proxmox API (pvesh over SSH to the node), idempotent by hostname, then
applies the manifest\[aq]s add-ons, files and provision scripts inside it.

Real trigger (2026-08-24, Spencer): \[dq]build extensible deploy via api to
pve strat\[dq] -- tiny, bare-minimum LXC per core service (bastion, qmail
MTA, snmpd), matching docs/guides/PVE_CAPACITY_PLANNING_METHODOLOGY.md\[aq]s
step 5 (\[dq]a new service is a new manifest entry, not new deploy code\[dq]).

Never installs anything on the pve host itself -- pvesh is Proxmox\[aq]s
own real API-wrapper CLI, already there on any real Proxmox node; this
tool just calls it over SSH from wherever hee runs.

Manifest format (pve/services/<name>.yaml):
  hostname: bastion
  template: alpine-3.23-default_20260116_amd64.tar.xz
  cores: 1
  memory_mb: 256
  disk_gb: 4
  storage: ssd1
  bridge: vmbr0
  unprivileged: true
  addons: [agent-tooling]           # named apk sets from the org\[aq]s registry (--addons)
  agent: ci-triage                  # agent roster role, only for a container that runs an agent
  files:                            # committed files pushed INTO the container
    - {src: pve/lab-dhcp/dnsmasq.conf, dst: /etc/dnsmasq.conf, mode: \[dq]0644\[dq]}
    - {generate: agent-roster-model, dst: /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.d/50-model.json, mode: \[dq]0644\[dq]}
  provision:                        # committed POSIX sh scripts run inside it
    - pve/lab-dhcp/provision.sh
  resolver:                         # the container\[aq]s /etc/resolv.conf, and nothing overwrites it
    nameservers: [10.0.0.194, 10.0.0.72]
    search: [lab.tcos.us, tcos.us]
    ndots: 2

\[ga]files:\[ga] and \[ga]provision:\[ga] paths are relative to the root of the repo the
manifest lives in (git rev-parse --show-toplevel), never to the current
directory and never absolute: a manifest names what it ships, and what it
ships is under review beside it. Real trigger (2026-09-10): the only
precedent for configuring what runs INSIDE a container was a README of
\[ga]pct exec N -- sh -c \[dq]cat > /etc/...\[dq]\[ga] one-liners (fleet-ops
pve/snmp-joe/README.md) -- the same imperative shape pve/network.yaml was
written to remove for bridges. Order: create -> addons -> files ->
provision. The manifest is fully resolved BEFORE the container is created,
so a missing file fails closed rather than leaving a half-provisioned
container behind. Every step is previewed under --dry-run, including
addons, which the dry run used to skip.

A \[ga]files:\[ga] entry names either \[ga]src\[ga] (a committed file) or \[ga]generate\[ga] (a
file rendered at deploy time, never committed). The one generator today is
\[ga]agent-roster-model\[ga]: it reads the org\[aq]s agent roster (--agent-roster), refuses unless
the agent roster is \[ga]status: ratified\[ga] AND its detached signature verifies with a
key the agent roster\[aq]s own ratification_evidence names, then renders the
Claude Code managed-settings drop-in that pins the manifest\[aq]s \[ga]agent:\[ga] to
the model the agent roster assigns. Real trigger (2026-09-10): the first agent
manifests shipped five hand-kept managed-settings.json copies, each
restating a model the signed agent roster already names -- a second source that
would leave every container on the old model after an agent roster change, with
nothing to say so. Operator: \[dq]generate from roster at deploy is correct,
footgun\[dq]. The model is named in exactly one place, and the dry run prints
the rendered file.

Every command run inside the container gets
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin. \[ga]pct exec\[ga]
otherwise passes PVE\[aq]s own /sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin, which has no
/usr/local/bin, so a provision script calling a tool it just linked there
exited 127 (measured 2026-09-10).

\[ga]resolver:\[ga] declares the container\[aq]s DNS: up to three IPv4 nameservers, in
order; an optional search list; and ndots (default 1). The tool writes
/etc/resolv.conf from it right after the network is up, before add-ons, and
makes it stick. Nothing else does, measured 2026-09-10: the golden template
sets no resolver, pve rewrites /etc/resolv.conf from the HOST\[aq]s file at every
container start, and Alpine\[aq]s udhcpc rewrites it from DHCP on every lease --
ct113 was resolving through 10.0.0.72 and 1.1.1.1 handed out by the
household router, and a public resolver answers NXDOMAIN for lab names. So
it also writes /etc/.pve-ignore.resolv.conf (pve skips the file), and for a
DHCP container /etc/udhcpc/udhcpc.conf with RESOLV_CONF=\[dq]no\[dq]. ndots matters
on musl: the search list is used only when a name has FEWER dots than
ndots, so \[ga]view.lab\[ga] (one dot) resolves as view.lab.tcos.us only with
ndots 2. Operator: \[dq]proper search and ndot settings too (view.lab/foo should
work)\[dq].
\f[R]
.fi
